Predicting the int8 GEMM's cycles from its machine code, and what the gap to the
measurement means
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2, llvm-aie
22.0.0.2026090201). NO NPU SESSION was built and no hardware context was taken: this reads two
already-compiled kernel objects out of ~/.npu/cache and computes. The measured microseconds it
is compared against were taken on 2026-09-07 and are quoted from
results/aie/int8_matmul_sweep_npu.log, not re-run.

Commands (from the repo root, in the ironenv):
  python tools/gemm_cost_model.py ~/.npu/cache/e9ed1c603d42d8bb153dfb76/matmul_i8_i32_86901378.o \
      --tile 64,64,64 --measured-us 7458.1
  python tools/gemm_cost_model.py ~/.npu/cache/0816364bbbaf03f83e2f0bcd/matmul_i8_i32_170456c5.o \
      --tile 64,64,32 --measured-us 14394.5

WHY: results/aie/aie2_isa_static.log established that a hardware-loop body's bundle count is its
cycle count on AIE2, and results/aie/pmu_probe_npu.log established that a buffer costs a core a
constant number of cycles on top of its work. Those two together are enough to compute a
kernel's issuing time from its object file, WITHOUT running it. Doing that and subtracting the
answer from a measured time gives the cycles the core spent not issuing, which is the quantity
every losing verdict in this repo has been missing. The int8 GEMM is the test case because it is
the strongest NPU result here (4607.05 GOPS) and because the same log rates its inner loop as
essentially perfectly scheduled, so whatever costs it the other two thirds is not the schedule.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0. A CORRECTION TO results/aie/aie2_isa_static.log, SECTION 5
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
That log's section 5 is headed "the kernel behind results/aie/int8_matmul_sweep_npu.log's
4607.05 GOPS" and disassembles
  ~/.npu/cache/0816364bbbaf03f83e2f0bcd/matmul_i8_i32_170456c5.o

That object is NOT the kernel behind 4607.05 GOPS. Its cache carries memref<64x32xi8> and
memref<64x32xi32> buffers, so it is the DEFAULT m=64 k=64 n=32 build, which measured 2387.01
GOPS on that shape. The 4607.05 row is the tuned n=64 build, whose kernel object is a different
hash, matmul_i8_i32_86901378.o, in eleven caches including e9ed1c603d42d8bb153dfb76.

The provenance line is wrong. Every NUMBER in that section is unaffected, because the n=64
object's loop is identical to the n=32 object's: nine bundles, eight vmac, accumulators cm0
through cm7, same 88.9% MAC issue density. The disassembly below shows it. The original log
stands as evidence of what was run and is not edited; this section supersedes its attribution.

