--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. READING: THE CORE TAKES THE SAME TIME PER BUFFER WHATEVER IS IN IT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Side by side, 4096x2048x2048 int8 on 16 cores, the two tiles measured in the same sitting:

  tile          vmac      issuing cycles   predicted    measured   issuing   measured
                per call     per call        us            us      fraction  cycles/call
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  m64 k64 n64      1024          1323        3014.5      7458.1      40.4%        3274
  m64 k64 n32       512           738        3364.2     14394.5      23.4%        3160

Three things follow and the third is the finding.

FIRST, the model closes against the source log's own throughput figure without being fitted to
it. The schedule term and the issuing term multiply to the measured fraction of peak in both
rows: 77.4% x 40.4% = 31.3%, and 4607.0 GOPS over the 14,732 GOPS those 16 cores can issue at
1.7983 GHz is 31.3%. For n=32, 69.3% x 23.4% = 16.2%, and 2387.0/14732 = 16.2%. The loop
iteration count also lands on a whole number, 126.0 exactly, which it would not if the MAC
geometry (4x8x8 for every int8 input on AIE2, from _MM_MAC_DIMS in the compiler's own
linalg.py) or the peeling count were wrong.

SECOND, the schedule is not where the GEMM loses. Over the whole call -- loop, software-pipeline
prologue and epilogue, amortised output zeroing and the ObjectFifo handoff -- the n=64 kernel
issues 198.1 MACs per cycle against the 256 the tile can retire, 77.4%. The remaining 22.6% is
mostly the 135 bundles of prologue and epilogue that run once per call and are dominated by
reloading the eight accumulators the register file cannot keep (416-byte frame, 33 stack
references). That is worth something, but it is not the missing factor of three.

THIRD, and this is the mechanism: THE MEASURED CYCLES PER CALL ARE THE SAME FOR BOTH TILES.
3,274 against 3,160, a 3.6% difference, for buffers whose compute differs by 2x. A core handed
twice the work per buffer finishes in the same wall time. That is not what an issue-bound
design does; it is what a design does when the time per buffer is set by getting the buffer
there. The 1,323 issuing cycles the n=64 call needs fit inside a 3,274-cycle slot with 1,951
cycles to spare, and the n=32 call's 738 fit inside 3,160 with 2,422 to spare.

That also explains the tile result the source log reported without a mechanism. Going from n=32
to n=64 measured 1.93x faster. The cost model says the two should differ by 12% in issuing time
(3364 vs 3015 us). The 1.93x therefore comes almost entirely from doing twice the work inside a
per-buffer slot whose length barely moved.

HOW STRONG IS THE 40.4%. The one constant carried over from another kernel is the ObjectFifo
handoff, 15 cycles per acquire/release pair. The pessimistic alternative is to charge the
probe's entire measured 717 cycles per buffer instead, even though 512 of those were that
probe's own trace-flush loop, which production code does not contain, and 190 were its kernel
prologue, which is counted here from the GEMM's own disassembly instead. Charging the full 717
per fifo raises the prediction to 2,727 cycles per call and the issuing fraction to 83.3% --
and even then 547 cycles per call, 16.7%, remain unaccounted. So the core is starved for
somewhere between 17% and 60% of the dispatch, and the low end of that range is the one built
on a constant that does not transfer.

WHAT THIS PREDICTS, AND HOW TO FALSIFY IT
Bytes into one core's L1 per call are 8,192 at n=64 and 6,144 at n=32. Divided by the measured
cycles per call, the core is being fed at 2.50 and 1.94 bytes per cycle. The never-starved rate
would need 6.19 and 8.32. So:

  H9. Stream-port tracing on this design will measure a sustained input rate at or below
      2.5 bytes per cycle into a core. Fails if the ports read faster than that, which would
      move the missing time somewhere other than input bandwidth.

  H10. Per-buffer wall time on this design is a constant set by the data path, so throughput
      rises with work per buffer until the issuing time approaches roughly 3,200 cycles.
      At n=64 the kernel uses 1,323 of those, leaving about 2.4x of headroom. Fails if a
      larger tile does not raise throughput.

H10 has a known obstacle already on record: results/aie/int8_matmul_sweep_npu.log's PROBE A
(m=128, n=64) and PROBE B (k=128, n=64) both failed to build with "'aie.tile' op Basic
sequential allocation failed", which is an L1 capacity limit, not a performance result. So the
headroom this model identifies is currently unreachable through the tile parameters alone, and
reaching it means changing what occupies L1 -- buffer depth, or the 16 KB single-buffered
output tile -- rather than asking for a bigger tile.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Nothing here was measured on hardware in this run. The issuing-cycle predictions are computed
  from object code; the microsecond figures they are compared against come from a run two days
  earlier under conditions documented in that log, not this one.
- The 15-cycle acquire/release constant was measured on a one-core, one-fifo probe with a
  different kernel. Its transfer to a two-fifo GEMM is an assumption, bounded above in section 3
  but not measured.
- "Not issuing" is a residual, not an observation. It is consistent with lock stall, which is
  the only stall category results/aie/pmu_probe_npu.log has yet seen non-zero, but this run
  does not attribute it. A port or stall trace on this design is what would.
- The 135 once-per-call bundles are assumed to execute once. That holds if the function has no
  branch other than its one hardware loop, which the disassembly is consistent with but which
  was not proved by walking every branch target.
- One power mode (Default, 1.7983 GHz), one dtype, one design. The conv kernels are untouched.
